{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"# Plugin architecture

This design uses a registry-controlled, signed, out-of-process plugin model. Plugins communicate with the reporting host through a versioned RPC contract and receive capabilities through a policy-enforced broker. Plugin upgrades use blue/green replacement so failed reloads do not interrupt existing reports.

## 1. Architecture

```text
                         Control plane
┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌────────────────┐
│ Plugin CLI   │──▶│ Plugin       │──▶│ Artifact / OCI │
│ and SDK      │   │ Registry     │   │ Registry       │
└──────────────┘   └──────┬───────┘   └────────────────┘
                           │
                    ┌──────▼──────┐
                    │ Policy and  │
                    │ deployment  │
                    │ controller  │
                    └──────┬──────┘
                           │
                         Data plane
┌──────────────────────────▼──────────────────────────┐
│ Reporting host                                        │
│                                                      │
│  Plugin catalog → Supervisor → Capability broker     │
│                         │                            │
│                local RPC / named pipe                │
│                         │                            │
│       ┌─────────────────▼─────────────────┐          │
│       │ Sandboxed plugin runner           │          │
│       │                                  │          │
│       │ Plugin code + runtime             │          │
│       └──────────────────────────────────┘          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The reporting host must never dynamically load arbitrary plugin code into its own process. Even trusted plugins run behind the same boundary.

## 2. Plugin types

Each plugin declares one primary type:

| Type | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `source` | Retrieves report data from an external system |
| `transformer` | Filters, maps, joins, aggregates, or enriches data |
| `validator` | Validates report definitions or data |
| `renderer` | Converts report data into a visual representation |
| `exporter` | Produces PDF, CSV, XLSX, JSON, or another output |
| `destination` | Sends reports to email, storage, chat, or an external service |
| `auth-provider` | Supplies authentication or authorization integration |
| `scheduler` | Provides external scheduling or event triggers |

A plugin may expose multiple capabilities, but each capability must use a separately versioned contract.

## 3. Registry

The registry is the source of truth for plugin identity, releases, permissions, compatibility, and deployment state.

### Plugin identity

Plugin IDs use reverse-DNS naming:

```text
com.example.salesforce-source
com.example.finance.pdf-exporter
org.acme.slack-destination
```

An ID is permanent. It must never be reused by another team or for an unrelated plugin.

### Registry entities

#### Plugin

```text
id
display_name
description
owner_team
technical_owner
support_contact
classification
created_at
status: active | deprecated | revoked
```

#### Release

```text
plugin_id
version
manifest
artifact_digest
signature
signing_key_id
sbom_digest
provenance_attestation
published_at
channel: dev | beta | stable
status: published | quarantined | revoked
minimum_host_version
api_major
api_min_minor
api_max_minor
```

Releases are immutable. Publishing another artifact under an existing version is prohibited.

#### Installation

```text
installation_id
tenant_id
plugin_id
selected_version
configuration
granted_permissions
state
channel
installed_at
updated_at
```

#### Runtime instance

```text
instance_id
installation_id
artifact_digest
config_hash
state: starting | ready | draining | failed | stopped
runner_id
started_at
last_health_check
```

### Registry operations

The registry should expose:

```http
POST   /v1/plugins
POST   /v1/plugins/{id}/releases
POST   /v1/plugins/{id}/releases/{version}/promote
GET    /v1/plugins
GET    /v1/plugins/{id}
GET    /v1/plugins/{id}/releases
POST   /v1/installations
PATCH  /v1/installations/{id}
POST   /v1/installations/{id}/enable
POST   /v1/installations/{id}/disable
POST   /v1/installations/{id}/reload
POST   /v1/installations/{id}/rollback
GET    /v1/audit-events
```

The registry must support filtering by:

- plugin type;
- capability;
- API compatibility;
- required permissions;
- tenant classification;
- channel;
- minimum host version;
- status.

### Version resolution

The resolver selects a release in this order:

1. Plugin ID and requested capability match.
2. Release is not revoked or quarantined.
3. Signature and artifact digest verify.
4. Host API version is within the plugin’s declared range.
5. Required permissions satisfy deployment policy.
6. Dependencies resolve.
7. Channel policy is satisfied.
8. Highest permitted version is selected.
9. The selected version is written to an installation lock.

Automatic upgrades must not change the lock without an explicit rollout policy.

